WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.068
-Our next guest is an
Academy Award-nominated director

00:00:03.068 --> 00:00:06.272
of more than 70 feature
and documentary films.

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.241
His novel "The Twilight World"

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:11.010
was published earlier this month
and is on sale now.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:14.213
Please welcome to the show
Werner Herzog, everybody!

00:00:14.213 --> 00:00:16.182
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:22.455 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:28.695 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:31.297
I'm absolutely delighted
to have you here, Werner.

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:33.333
-Thank you.
Thank you for having me.

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:36.769
-So, this is a novel based on
a true story about

00:00:36.769 --> 00:00:39.939
a Japanese soldier who was
stationed in the Philippines

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:43.242
and stayed for 30 years
after the war ended

00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:45.845
because he believed that
that was his duty.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:48.648
-Not only his duty,
he had proof

00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:53.318
or he concocted partially proof
that the war was still on.

00:00:53.318 --> 00:00:55.087
He firmly believed in it.

00:00:55.087 --> 00:00:58.825
And he derived it from,
for example, leaflets were

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:02.962
thrown down and there were some
characters were misspelled.

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:07.166
So he believed it must have been
the enemy, the CIA,

00:01:07.166 --> 00:01:10.536
who couldn't properly write
Japanese characters.

00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:14.207
Then he saw massive amounts of

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:17.811
aircraft flying
overhead to the west.

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:20.513
But that was already
the Korean War.

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.081
So the war hadn't ended.

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:24.951
And he actually was shot at.

00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:30.423
He survived 111 ambushes
and skirmishes,

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:33.926
while his last two comrades
were shot dead.

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:38.130
So there was proof enough
for him the war is still on.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.833
-Now, explain how you first
met --

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:42.569
I want to get the pronunciation
right.

00:01:42.569 --> 00:01:44.437
Hiroo? How do you say
the soldier's name?

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:46.672
-Hiroo Onoda.
-Hiroo Onoda.

00:01:46.672 --> 00:01:48.874
So, you actually met him
in 1997?

00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:50.810
-Yes.
-How did this meeting come
about?

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.413
-It's an embarrassment,
but I'll confess.

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:54.747
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:57.550
What happened is
I staged an opera.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:02.021
Sometimes I do odd jobs,
like acting or I do operas.

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:06.125
[ Laughter ]
So, the composer --

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:09.395
It was a world premiere of
a modern composition.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:13.699
The composer, very famous in
Japan, he had his own TV show.

00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:17.036
He came and people knew
what we were doing.

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:21.507
He comes one night, completely
excited at a table where

00:02:21.507 --> 00:02:24.843
we all had dinner, and he said,
"Herzog-san, you know what?

00:02:24.843 --> 00:02:28.882
The office of the emperor
stretched out feelers,"

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:32.785
when I was ready to meet the
emperor in a private audience.

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:34.887
And I immediately said,
"Oh, my God,

00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:38.357
it will be only formalities
and pleasantries.

00:02:38.357 --> 00:02:40.159
That's not my kind of thing."

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:43.029
And this was a faux pas so bad.

00:02:43.029 --> 00:02:47.533
I mean, in Japan, it's
so unbelievably bad.

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:51.571
Until today, I wish the floor
would open

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:53.439
right here and swallow me up.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:55.107
It's such an embarrassment.

00:02:55.107 --> 00:02:58.344
And there was silence.
Silent, silent silence.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:02.047
Nobody made eye contact with me
anymore, it was so bad.

00:03:02.047 --> 00:03:06.352
And somebody asked, "Whom else,
if not the emperor,

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:07.787
would you like to meet?"

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:09.288
And I said, "Onoda."

00:03:09.288 --> 00:03:10.823
And they said, "Onoda, Onoda?"

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:13.092
"Yes, Hiroo Onoda."

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:17.430
And very few days later, I met
him, and I met him a few times.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.065
We had very, very deep
conversations

00:03:20.065 --> 00:03:22.401
because he lived in the jungle.

00:03:22.401 --> 00:03:26.872
I have worked in the jungle,
in a different one, in Amazonia.

00:03:26.872 --> 00:03:32.511
-Yes.
-So we had very, very deep
and instant rapport.

00:03:32.511 --> 00:03:37.516
-I find you to be both very
honest and yet also cryptic.

00:03:37.516 --> 00:03:39.051
Is that fair?

00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:41.854
And you, the opening --
there was sort of in

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:45.558
the opening page, this I found
to be very Herzogian.

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:46.859
You wrote in the beginning,

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:49.595
"Most details are factually
correct, some are not.

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:50.963
What was important to the author

00:03:50.963 --> 00:03:52.565
was something other than
accuracy,

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:55.367
some essence he thought he
glimpsed when he encountered

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:57.202
the protagonist of this story."

00:03:57.202 --> 00:04:00.974
-Sure, I have to make
a statement because I was not

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:02.875
there when he was in the jungle.

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:06.979
And for example,
I am quoting dialogues

00:04:06.979 --> 00:04:09.215
he had with his few comrades.

00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:11.884
He had three,
and one deserted very soon.

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:14.253
Two left,
and they were shot dead.

00:04:14.253 --> 00:04:19.092
So I'm describing dialogues,
but I haven't been there.

00:04:19.092 --> 00:04:21.427
I only know the gist of it.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:24.630
And I had to invent
to a certain degree.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:30.536
So, of course, I have not been
an eyewitness.

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:34.207
Otherwise, I wouldn't do
a caveat like that.

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:38.377
But today, you see, we have
too many fake news,

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:43.115
and you have to tell
what is happening

00:04:43.115 --> 00:04:45.451
and what is my own invention.

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:49.555
And sometimes I would invent
a few things in order

00:04:49.555 --> 00:04:52.391
to make some essence
of the man visible.

00:04:52.391 --> 00:04:55.728
His dignity, his stoicism,

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:58.931
his perseverance,

00:04:58.931 --> 00:05:02.502
his dreams, his nightmares.

00:05:02.502 --> 00:05:07.139
So all this has to be
sometimes invented.

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:10.343
-You have another book
coming out very soon.

00:05:10.343 --> 00:05:12.278
You have two more films
coming out.

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:13.946
You've always been
very prolific.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:17.082
Are you someone, while you're
working, that you feel the work

00:05:17.082 --> 00:05:19.151
propels you
to your next project?

00:05:19.151 --> 00:05:20.986
-It's hard to describe.

00:05:20.986 --> 00:05:23.389
Sometimes I --
Or most of the time,

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:25.524
they come with great vehemence
at me.

00:05:25.524 --> 00:05:27.793
And so I have to man up,
and I say,

00:05:27.793 --> 00:05:29.595
"Yes, I'm going to face this
now."

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:33.599
But what happened is,
four things came at once.

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:35.668
So two books, two films.

00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:37.836
And all of this will be released

00:05:37.836 --> 00:05:41.107
within the next
six, eight weeks.

00:05:41.107 --> 00:05:44.677
A new film in the United Kingdom

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:47.480
in four days from now
or five days from now.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.116
I can't even be there
at the world premiere.

00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:54.187
I just flew in from Europe,
so I'm not going to crisscross,

00:05:54.187 --> 00:05:55.687
so I miss my own world premiere.

00:05:55.687 --> 00:05:58.324
-Do you get nervous about when
a new movie of yours comes out?

00:05:58.324 --> 00:06:01.193
-No, no, no, I have --
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.193 --> 00:06:04.230
-You don't strike me as someone
who often gets nervous.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:08.233
-No, I've seen it all,
and I've done wild stuff,

00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:13.105
so you can't -- you can't knock
me out of my shoes.

00:06:13.105 --> 00:06:15.341
[ Laughter ]
-I want to ask a question.

00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:18.277
My mother is a French teacher.
I know you're multilingual.

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:20.379
I once heard you say
that you speak French.

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:22.448
You don't like speaking French.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:25.517
You only spoke it once.

00:06:25.517 --> 00:06:26.852
-Yes.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:26.852 --> 00:06:28.921
No, actually twice.

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:33.425
Once on a volcano that was about
to explode in the Caribbean.

00:06:33.425 --> 00:06:37.696
And I filmed with the last man
who actually lived on the slope

00:06:37.696 --> 00:06:40.766
of the volcano, a poor farmer
in Guadeloupe,

00:06:40.766 --> 00:06:42.000
and it's French-speaking.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.036
They speak Creole French.

00:06:44.036 --> 00:06:46.739
I even had to wake him up
on camera,

00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:49.809
and the mountain right on
top of us was --

00:06:49.809 --> 00:06:52.377
gave signals
it was going to explode.

00:06:52.377 --> 00:06:54.046
So I had to speak French.

00:06:54.046 --> 00:06:56.983
-So basically only because
a volcano was erupting?

00:06:56.983 --> 00:06:58.650
[ Laughter ]

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:04.189
-No, because he was the only man
who didn't want to be evacuated,

00:07:04.189 --> 00:07:05.758
so I had to speak French.

00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:09.494
The other time,
in the Central African Republic,

00:07:09.494 --> 00:07:12.465
I was passing through, arrested
a couple of times

00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:15.468
by drunk soldiers
in the middle of the night.

00:07:15.468 --> 00:07:20.205
And one gun here, one gun at my
heart, one gun somewhere there.

00:07:20.205 --> 00:07:21.840
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:24.810
-And I tried to explain that we
were not soldiers,

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.545
we were not mercenaries.

00:07:26.545 --> 00:07:29.314
We were doing a film.
And they said to me --

00:07:29.314 --> 00:07:32.251
And I tried French and German
and Spanish and whatever.

00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:35.020
And they said...
[ Speaking French ]

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:36.922
"We speak French here."

00:07:36.922 --> 00:07:39.424
So I had to do some French.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:39.424 --> 00:07:42.160
-Yeah. And do you regret it
a little bit, even though --

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:44.897
-No, no, no, it's okay now.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:44.897 --> 00:07:47.867
I have no problem with the
French and French language.

00:07:47.867 --> 00:07:51.169
It's wonderful. But when you
talk with French people,

00:07:51.169 --> 00:07:56.509
they love to go into irony,
into the mood of irony.

00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:58.510
And I have a speech defect.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:01.447
I have a communication defect.

00:08:01.447 --> 00:08:04.417
I do not understand irony.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:04.417 --> 00:08:06.586
You can do the wildest stuff
with me.

00:08:06.586 --> 00:08:08.954
I always understand it verbatim.

00:08:08.954 --> 00:08:12.792
Humor, yes.
Humor, I completely understand.

00:08:12.792 --> 00:08:15.828
There's a lot of humor
in all of my films,

00:08:15.828 --> 00:08:18.230
even when I'm playing a villain,

00:08:18.230 --> 00:08:23.068
a real badass bad guy,
like in "Jack Reacher."

00:08:23.068 --> 00:08:25.570
It has a sort of humor in it.

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:28.207
-It's wonderful when we see you
playing those roles

00:08:28.207 --> 00:08:30.442
that are so unexpected,
like "Jack Reacher."

00:08:30.442 --> 00:08:33.846
It must be fun to do.
-Yes.

00:08:33.846 --> 00:08:39.251
I was really invited
with great enthusiasm

00:08:39.251 --> 00:08:43.522
by Christopher McQuarrie,
the director, and Tom Cruise.

00:08:43.522 --> 00:08:47.059
They really wanted to have me
there because they've seen me

00:08:47.059 --> 00:08:49.494
in other films
by Harmony Korine,

00:08:49.494 --> 00:08:54.132
where I play a dysfunctional
father who is completely hostile

00:08:54.132 --> 00:08:57.336
with his children and terrorizes
the family.

00:08:57.336 --> 00:09:00.005
They had seen that,
and they knew I probably would

00:09:00.005 --> 00:09:02.141
be good to scare the audience.

00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:03.575
[ Laughter ]

00:09:03.575 --> 00:09:06.845
And now it sounds funny.

00:09:06.845 --> 00:09:10.015
Yes, I was scary.
I was really scary.

00:09:10.015 --> 00:09:12.118
But it's a performance.

00:09:12.118 --> 00:09:15.654
As a private person,
I'm kind of friendly.

00:09:15.654 --> 00:09:20.426
My wife will testify
that I'm sweet and fluffy.

00:09:20.426 --> 00:09:21.626
[ Laughter ]

00:09:21.626 --> 00:09:22.894
-We might have to check
with her.

00:09:22.894 --> 00:09:24.463
-I'm a fluffy husband.

00:09:24.463 --> 00:09:25.965
[ Laughter ]

00:09:25.965 --> 00:09:28.434
-I have one last question,
because you're multilingual.

00:09:28.434 --> 00:09:31.670
I've had some people -- some
German viewers of the show

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:33.572
take issue with the fact --
I think it's very American

00:09:33.572 --> 00:09:36.408
that you assume the English
expression is the only one.

00:09:36.408 --> 00:09:39.011
So, here, we say roosters say
"cock-a-doodle-doo."

00:09:39.011 --> 00:09:41.413
-Yeah.
-Germany, what would they say?

00:09:41.413 --> 00:09:42.915
-Kikeriki.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:45.017
-Which do you think is closer?

00:09:45.017 --> 00:09:49.021
-Well, the English is far remote
because they had the Brexit.

00:09:49.021 --> 00:09:52.358
-Yeah.
-So the British rooster is

00:09:52.358 --> 00:09:55.961
somewhere out in the Atlantic.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:55.961 --> 00:09:58.030
-Yeah, that's a good way of
looking at it.

00:09:58.030 --> 00:09:59.865
Let them have
"cock-a-doodle-doo."

00:09:59.865 --> 00:10:01.466
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:10:01.466 --> 00:10:02.601
It's a wonderful book.
-Thank you.

00:10:02.601 --> 00:10:04.803
-Congratulations on everything.

00:10:04.803 --> 00:10:06.472
Werner Herzog, everybody!

00:10:06.472 --> 00:10:08.607
"The Twilight World" is
available wherever

00:10:08.607 --> 00:10:09.975
you buy books, so please support

00:10:09.975 --> 00:10:11.410
local and independent
bookstores.

